1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:03,403
Here is the question this workshop answers.

2
00:00:03,403 --> 00:00:07,725
How do we build a secure, repeatable Kubernetes platform on AWS,

3
00:00:07,725 --> 00:00:12,721
where a developer just pushes code, and that code is automatically tested,

4
00:00:12,721 --> 00:00:16,300
security scanned, signed, and safely deployed to EKS?

5
00:00:16,833 --> 00:00:19,456
Every push goes through the same checks:

6
00:00:19,456 --> 00:00:22,866
leaked secrets, unit tests, vulnerable dependencies,

7
00:00:22,866 --> 00:00:27,588
the Dockerfile, the infrastructure code, and the container image itself.

8
00:00:28,133 --> 00:00:35,263
Only then is the image deployed to Amazon EKS, by its exact digest, behind an AWS load balancer.

9
00:00:35,800 --> 00:00:40,380
And all of that happens without a single AWS access key stored in GitHub.

10
00:00:40,933 --> 00:00:47,331
I will build the whole thing with you on a real AWS account, from a fresh clone of the repository.

11
00:00:47,331 --> 00:00:49,616
In part one, we build the platform.

12
00:00:49,616 --> 00:00:52,684
In part two, we ship code through the pipeline,

13
00:00:52,684 --> 00:00:57,188
break things on purpose, troubleshoot them, and tear everything down.

14
00:00:57,188 --> 00:00:58,625
Let us start with why.

15
00:01:00,367 --> 00:01:03,075
Let us be concrete about the problems.

16
00:01:03,075 --> 00:01:06,710
First, long lived AWS keys stored in the CI system.

17
00:01:06,710 --> 00:01:11,771
If one leaks, an attacker has your cloud account until someone notices.

18
00:01:12,333 --> 00:01:14,634
Second, security as a final step.

19
00:01:14,634 --> 00:01:20,978
Someone scans the application after it is already in production, or nobody scans it at all.

20
00:01:21,533 --> 00:01:24,907
Third, nobody knows what is inside the container image.

21
00:01:24,907 --> 00:01:28,159
When the next big library vulnerability is announced,

22
00:01:28,159 --> 00:01:31,840
answering 'are we affected?' takes days instead of a search.

23
00:01:32,400 --> 00:01:35,262
Fourth, no proof of where an image came from.

24
00:01:35,262 --> 00:01:39,014
Anyone who can push to the registry can put an image there.

25
00:01:39,567 --> 00:01:44,106
Fifth, containers that run as root, with full Linux capabilities.

26
00:01:44,106 --> 00:01:48,016
One application bug, and the attacker controls the node.

27
00:01:48,567 --> 00:01:52,901
And sixth, infrastructure built by clicking around in the console.

28
00:01:52,901 --> 00:01:56,578
Nobody can review it, and nobody can rebuild it exactly.

29
00:01:58,333 --> 00:02:02,337
So each tool in this project answers one of those problems.

30
00:02:02,337 --> 00:02:04,508
EKS gives us managed Kubernetes:

31
00:02:04,508 --> 00:02:09,258
AWS runs and patches the control plane, and we focus on our workloads.

32
00:02:09,800 --> 00:02:12,557
Terraform turns the whole platform into code:

33
00:02:12,557 --> 00:02:17,460
reviewed in a pull request, rebuilt identically, and destroyed with one command.

34
00:02:18,000 --> 00:02:21,887
DevSecOps means the security checks live inside the pipeline.

35
00:02:21,887 --> 00:02:26,540
A problem is caught minutes after the push, not months after the release.

36
00:02:27,100 --> 00:02:29,629
Image scanning catches known vulnerabilities,

37
00:02:29,629 --> 00:02:34,686
the SBOM lists every component, and the signature proves the image came from our pipeline.

38
00:02:35,233 --> 00:02:41,423
GitHub OIDC replaces stored AWS keys with temporary credentials, valid for one job.

39
00:02:41,967 --> 00:02:44,624
And the workload itself runs restricted:

40
00:02:44,624 --> 00:02:50,536
non-root, a read-only file system, no Linux capabilities, and network policies around it.

41
00:02:52,300 --> 00:02:55,884
Before we create anything, here is the complete picture.

42
00:02:55,884 --> 00:02:59,019
A developer pushes code to the GitHub repository.

43
00:02:59,567 --> 00:03:02,655
GitHub Actions runs the security checks first:

44
00:03:02,655 --> 00:03:05,474
gitleaks for secrets, lint and unit tests,

45
00:03:05,474 --> 00:03:11,852
npm audit, hadolint for the Dockerfile, and a Trivy scan of the Terraform and Kubernetes files.

46
00:03:11,852 --> 00:03:14,201
It even checks the pipeline itself.

47
00:03:14,767 --> 00:03:21,571
Only then does it build the Docker image, scan it with Trivy, generate an SBOM, and sign it with cosign.

48
00:03:22,133 --> 00:03:29,573
The image goes to Amazon ECR, the container registry, and it is referenced by its digest, never just a tag.

49
00:03:30,133 --> 00:03:32,863
EKS runs it with security restrictions:

50
00:03:32,863 --> 00:03:39,933
Pod Security, network policies, resource limits, health probes, two replicas and a disruption budget.

51
00:03:40,467 --> 00:03:47,381
And the AWS Load Balancer Controller turns our Kubernetes Ingress into an Application Load Balancer with a public URL.

52
00:03:49,133 --> 00:03:51,124
And here is the AWS side.

53
00:03:51,124 --> 00:03:54,468
Everything lives in one region, Frankfurt.

54
00:03:55,033 --> 00:03:57,328
A VPC with two availability zones.

55
00:03:57,328 --> 00:04:01,581
Public subnets hold only the load balancer and the NAT gateway.

56
00:04:01,581 --> 00:04:04,618
The two worker nodes live in private subnets,

57
00:04:04,618 --> 00:04:09,073
without public IP addresses, and VPC flow logs record the traffic.

58
00:04:09,633 --> 00:04:12,635
The EKS control plane is managed by AWS.

59
00:04:12,635 --> 00:04:17,663
It writes audit logs, and access is controlled with access entries.

60
00:04:18,200 --> 00:04:23,079
KMS encrypts the Kubernetes secrets, and ECR stores our images.

61
00:04:23,633 --> 00:04:25,414
CloudWatch keeps the logs.

62
00:04:25,414 --> 00:04:28,084
IAM is global, so we are careful there:

63
00:04:28,084 --> 00:04:32,055
we only create roles whose names start with eks-devsecops,

64
00:04:32,055 --> 00:04:36,848
and we only read the shared GitHub identity provider, never change it.

65
00:04:37,400 --> 00:04:40,351
The Application Load Balancer is the odd one out:

66
00:04:40,351 --> 00:04:42,098
Terraform does not create it.

67
00:04:42,098 --> 00:04:46,194
The load balancer controller creates it from our Kubernetes Ingress.

68
00:04:46,194 --> 00:04:50,109
Remember that, because it matters a lot when we tear things down.

69
00:04:51,867 --> 00:04:54,041
How do these parts talk to each other?

70
00:04:54,041 --> 00:04:56,502
Internet traffic reaches the load balancer,

71
00:04:56,502 --> 00:05:01,653
and the load balancer forwards it straight to the pod IP addresses in the private subnets.

72
00:05:02,200 --> 00:05:05,131
The nodes can reach out, through the NAT gateway,

73
00:05:05,131 --> 00:05:09,259
for example to pull images, but nothing on the internet can reach in.

74
00:05:09,800 --> 00:05:13,705
GitHub Actions talks to AWS with a short lived role from OIDC.

75
00:05:13,705 --> 00:05:18,114
That role can push to our container registry and describe our cluster.

76
00:05:18,667 --> 00:05:24,258
The same role reaches Kubernetes through an access entry, which limits it to a single namespace.

77
00:05:24,800 --> 00:05:29,979
And the load balancer controller gets its AWS permissions through EKS Pod Identity.

78
00:05:29,979 --> 00:05:33,099
No access keys are stored anywhere in the cluster.

79
00:05:34,833 --> 00:05:37,822
Here is the pipeline we are about to run.

80
00:05:37,822 --> 00:05:42,196
Notice how many stages are security stages, marked in amber.

81
00:05:42,196 --> 00:05:46,788
Secrets, dependencies, the Dockerfile, the infrastructure code.

82
00:05:47,333 --> 00:05:49,012
Then the image itself:

83
00:05:49,012 --> 00:05:52,599
a vulnerability scan, an SBOM, and a signature.

84
00:05:52,599 --> 00:05:55,957
A classic CI/CD pipeline builds and deploys.

85
00:05:55,957 --> 00:06:00,918
A DevSecOps pipeline also refuses to deploy what it cannot trust.

86
00:06:01,467 --> 00:06:03,589
And the deployment itself is verified:

87
00:06:03,589 --> 00:06:06,605
the exact digest is deployed, the rollout must finish,

88
00:06:06,605 --> 00:06:12,526
the pipeline proves its own permissions are limited, and a smoke test goes through the real load balancer.

89
00:06:14,267 --> 00:06:16,475
Let us start where you would start:

90
00:06:16,475 --> 00:06:17,800
clone the repository.

91
00:06:17,800 --> 00:06:23,163
Before we run anything, I want you to understand what we are actually about to build.

92
00:06:23,700 --> 00:06:25,892
The pipeline lives in two workflows:

93
00:06:25,892 --> 00:06:29,423
ci dot yaml with the security checks, and deploy dot yaml,

94
00:06:29,423 --> 00:06:32,954
which runs those checks first and then builds and deploys.

95
00:06:32,954 --> 00:06:36,181
CODEOWNERS and Dependabot handle reviews and updates.

96
00:06:36,733 --> 00:06:40,488
The terraform folder describes the whole AWS platform:

97
00:06:40,488 --> 00:06:44,381
the network, the cluster, the registry, the GitHub role,

98
00:06:44,381 --> 00:06:50,430
and the load balancer controller, with its official IAM policy pinned to version 3.5.0.

99
00:06:50,967 --> 00:06:53,993
The k8s folder holds the Kubernetes manifests:

100
00:06:53,993 --> 00:06:59,651
deployment, service, ingress, network policies, disruption budget and service account.

101
00:07:00,200 --> 00:07:06,480
The app folder holds the application, its Dockerfile, and the nginx configuration with the security headers.

102
00:07:07,033 --> 00:07:12,817
And the scripts folder holds the tool installer for CI, plus two scripts we will use at the very end:

103
00:07:12,817 --> 00:07:16,481
the teardown, and the verification that nothing was left behind.

104
00:07:16,481 --> 00:07:22,665
Dot trivyignore lists the security exceptions we accepted on purpose, each with a reason and an expiry date.

105
00:07:24,400 --> 00:07:26,669
The application is a profile card.

106
00:07:26,669 --> 00:07:28,470
It is intentionally simple.

107
00:07:28,470 --> 00:07:31,606
The goal of this project is not to teach React.

108
00:07:31,606 --> 00:07:36,945
The goal is to show a production style DevSecOps platform around an application.

109
00:07:37,500 --> 00:07:40,760
The original profile card was a single HTML file.

110
00:07:40,760 --> 00:07:44,885
For this project it is rebuilt as a small React app with Vite.

111
00:07:45,433 --> 00:07:46,398
Why rebuild it?

112
00:07:46,398 --> 00:07:49,937
Because a real build step gives the pipeline real work:

113
00:07:49,937 --> 00:07:54,955
a dependency tree to audit, unit tests to run, and a production build to scan.

114
00:07:55,500 --> 00:07:59,417
The page footer shows the version and git commit of the running build,

115
00:07:59,417 --> 00:08:01,655
which the pipeline bakes into the image.

116
00:08:01,655 --> 00:08:04,285
So you can always see exactly what is deployed.

117
00:08:06,033 --> 00:08:09,736
Before anything touches the cloud, the developer loop.

118
00:08:09,736 --> 00:08:15,360
npm ci installs exactly what the lock file says, and the linter finds no problems.

119
00:08:15,900 --> 00:08:17,251
Five unit tests pass.

120
00:08:17,251 --> 00:08:19,631
One of them checks a security detail:

121
00:08:19,631 --> 00:08:25,741
external links open with no referrer and no opener, so the linked site cannot control our page.

122
00:08:26,300 --> 00:08:29,757
npm audit with level high finds zero vulnerabilities.

123
00:08:29,757 --> 00:08:35,041
The pipeline runs exactly this command, and later we will see it fail on purpose.

124
00:08:35,600 --> 00:08:38,124
And the production build is tiny:

125
00:08:38,124 --> 00:08:42,254
one HTML file, one CSS file and one JavaScript bundle.

126
00:08:42,254 --> 00:08:44,855
This is all that nginx will serve.

127
00:08:46,600 --> 00:08:49,302
Now behave like a careful engineer:

128
00:08:49,302 --> 00:08:50,615
check your tools.

129
00:08:50,615 --> 00:08:57,486
Git, Terraform, the AWS CLI, Docker, kubectl version 1.37 to match the cluster, and Helm.

130
00:08:57,486 --> 00:09:04,821
kubectl only supports one minor version of difference from the cluster, so the version matters.

131
00:09:05,367 --> 00:09:08,920
Then the most important command before any cloud work:

132
00:09:08,920 --> 00:09:10,762
aws sts get caller identity.

133
00:09:10,762 --> 00:09:15,367
It tells you which account and which identity you are about to change.

134
00:09:15,367 --> 00:09:18,986
I have redacted mine, but you should always read yours.

135
00:09:19,533 --> 00:09:20,530
And the region.

136
00:09:20,530 --> 00:09:24,318
Every command in this workshop is pinned to eu-central-1.

137
00:09:24,318 --> 00:09:29,569
I built this in a shared company account, so we work inside a controlled scope:

138
00:09:29,569 --> 00:09:31,895
one region, our own resources only.

139
00:09:31,895 --> 00:09:35,683
Terraform enforces that too, as you will see in a moment.

140
00:09:37,433 --> 00:09:40,270
Before running Terraform, let us read it.

141
00:09:40,270 --> 00:09:44,213
Terraform is our source of truth for this infrastructure.

142
00:09:44,213 --> 00:09:45,527
First, guard rails.

143
00:09:45,527 --> 00:09:48,087
The region variable has a validation:

144
00:09:48,087 --> 00:09:50,923
anything other than Frankfurt is refused.

145
00:09:51,467 --> 00:09:53,637
The provider has allowed account IDs.

146
00:09:53,637 --> 00:09:56,747
I pass my account ID through an environment variable,

147
00:09:56,747 --> 00:10:01,968
and if my credentials ever pointed at a different account, Terraform would refuse to run.

148
00:10:02,533 --> 00:10:04,695
Every resource gets default tags:

149
00:10:04,695 --> 00:10:07,381
project, owner, and managed by Terraform.

150
00:10:07,381 --> 00:10:12,163
At the end, we will use these tags to prove that nothing was left behind.

151
00:10:12,700 --> 00:10:13,637
And the state.

152
00:10:13,637 --> 00:10:17,383
Terraform state is kept locally here and ignored by git,

153
00:10:17,383 --> 00:10:22,334
because it contains account IDs, resource details, sometimes even secrets.

154
00:10:22,334 --> 00:10:25,814
Never commit Terraform state to a public repository.

155
00:10:25,814 --> 00:10:31,701
For a team, you would put it in an S3 bucket with locking, which we discuss in part two.

156
00:10:33,467 --> 00:10:39,315
The network uses the community VPC module, pinned to version 6.7.3.

157
00:10:39,315 --> 00:10:45,861
One address range, 10.42 dot 0 dot 0 slash 16, over two availability zones.

158
00:10:46,400 --> 00:10:52,564
Two large private subnets for the nodes and pods, and two small public subnets for the load balancer only.

159
00:10:53,100 --> 00:10:57,096
One NAT gateway gives the private nodes outbound internet access.

160
00:10:57,096 --> 00:10:58,633
One is cheaper for a lab.

161
00:10:58,633 --> 00:11:04,289
In production you would use one per zone, so a zone failure does not cut off the other zone.

162
00:11:04,833 --> 00:11:11,223
These tags tell the load balancer controller which subnets it may use for internet facing and internal load balancers.

163
00:11:11,767 --> 00:11:13,213
And two security habits:

164
00:11:13,213 --> 00:11:19,241
the default security group allows nothing, and flow logs record every connection for investigations.

165
00:11:20,000 --> 00:11:25,715
The cluster uses the community EKS module, version 21.26.

166
00:11:25,715 --> 00:11:32,911
Kubernetes 1.37, the newest version in standard support, placed in the private subnets.

167
00:11:33,467 --> 00:11:37,411
The control plane writes API, audit and authenticator logs,

168
00:11:37,411 --> 00:11:40,888
and Kubernetes secrets are encrypted with a KMS key.

169
00:11:40,888 --> 00:11:47,506
I switched off IRSA, because we use the newer Pod Identity, which needs no extra identity provider.

170
00:11:48,067 --> 00:11:52,019
Access entries map AWS identities to Kubernetes permissions.

171
00:11:52,019 --> 00:11:57,816
Our GitHub deploy role gets the edit policy, but only inside the profile card namespace.

172
00:11:58,367 --> 00:12:05,136
The VPC CNI add-on gets network policy enforcement switched on, and the Pod Identity agent is installed.

173
00:12:05,700 --> 00:12:06,981
And the node group:

174
00:12:06,981 --> 00:12:14,124
t3 medium instances, instance metadata version two required, with a hop limit of one, and encrypted disks.

175
00:12:15,867 --> 00:12:20,744
This account already has a GitHub identity provider, shared by other teams.

176
00:12:20,744 --> 00:12:24,711
IAM is global, so Terraform only reads it with a data source.

177
00:12:24,711 --> 00:12:29,653
A switch exists for fresh accounts that have none, and it is off by default.

178
00:12:30,200 --> 00:12:32,750
The container registry has immutable tags,

179
00:12:32,750 --> 00:12:36,938
so a tag can never be overwritten, and every pushed image is scanned.

180
00:12:36,938 --> 00:12:40,094
Force delete lets the teardown remove it completely.

181
00:12:40,633 --> 00:12:46,557
The load balancer controller gets its AWS permissions through a Pod Identity association,

182
00:12:46,557 --> 00:12:49,152
for one service account in kube-system.

183
00:12:49,152 --> 00:12:49,685
No keys.

184
00:12:50,233 --> 00:12:55,424
And Terraform installs the controller itself with Helm, chart version 3.5.0.

185
00:12:55,424 --> 00:13:00,273
So one terraform apply gives us a cluster that is ready for an Ingress.

186
00:13:02,033 --> 00:13:05,823
Before we create anything, let us look at the account.

187
00:13:05,823 --> 00:13:08,841
In Frankfurt there is only the default VPC.

188
00:13:08,841 --> 00:13:14,246
No instances, no load balancers, no clusters, no NAT gateways, no registries.

189
00:13:14,246 --> 00:13:15,720
This is the baseline.

190
00:13:15,720 --> 00:13:22,598
At the very end we compare against it, so we can prove we left the account exactly as we found it.

191
00:13:23,133 --> 00:13:25,484
And one important existing thing:

192
00:13:25,484 --> 00:13:27,408
a GitHub identity provider.

193
00:13:27,408 --> 00:13:31,326
It belongs to the whole account, because IAM is global.

194
00:13:31,326 --> 00:13:33,677
Other pipelines may depend on it.

195
00:13:33,677 --> 00:13:35,672
We will reuse it, read only.

196
00:13:35,672 --> 00:13:39,163
We will not modify it, and we will not delete it.

197
00:13:40,900 --> 00:13:44,381
terraform init downloads the providers and modules.

198
00:13:44,381 --> 00:13:48,340
And on my first try it failed, which is worth showing you.

199
00:13:48,340 --> 00:13:54,551
On Windows, Terraform downloads modules with git, and git hit the 260 character path limit:

200
00:13:54,551 --> 00:13:55,779
filename too long.

201
00:13:56,333 --> 00:13:59,315
My working folder simply had a very long path.

202
00:13:59,315 --> 00:14:03,918
The fix is to work from a short folder, or to enable long paths in git.

203
00:14:04,467 --> 00:14:07,154
From a short folder, init works:

204
00:14:07,154 --> 00:14:15,301
the VPC, EKS and KMS modules, and the AWS, Helm and Kubernetes providers, all at pinned versions.

205
00:14:15,867 --> 00:14:19,870
terraform validate checks the configuration itself.

206
00:14:19,870 --> 00:14:20,341
Valid.

207
00:14:22,100 --> 00:14:23,113
Now the plan.

208
00:14:23,113 --> 00:14:25,920
I am not going to blindly run apply.

209
00:14:25,920 --> 00:14:30,519
First I want to see exactly what Terraform wants to create.

210
00:14:30,519 --> 00:14:31,454
The network:

211
00:14:31,454 --> 00:14:34,494
the VPC, four subnets, the NAT gateway.

212
00:14:35,033 --> 00:14:38,813
The cluster, the node group, and the KMS key.

213
00:14:39,367 --> 00:14:40,046
IAM roles:

214
00:14:40,046 --> 00:14:45,890
for the GitHub deploy pipeline, for the load balancer controller, and for the cluster.

215
00:14:45,890 --> 00:14:49,016
The registry, the log groups and the flow log.

216
00:14:49,016 --> 00:14:52,821
And inside Kubernetes, the controller and our namespace.

217
00:14:53,367 --> 00:14:55,080
And the most important line:

218
00:14:55,080 --> 00:14:58,263
seventy two to add, zero to change, zero to destroy.

219
00:14:58,263 --> 00:15:06,341
In a shared account, zero to change and zero to destroy is your proof that you will not touch anything that belongs to someone else.

220
00:15:08,100 --> 00:15:10,765
Now we apply exactly that saved plan.

221
00:15:10,765 --> 00:15:13,934
The registry and the VPC take a second each.

222
00:15:14,500 --> 00:15:19,154
The KMS key takes half a minute, and the NAT gateway almost two minutes.

223
00:15:19,700 --> 00:15:22,193
The EKS control plane is the slow part:

224
00:15:22,193 --> 00:15:24,558
eight minutes and twenty one seconds.

225
00:15:24,558 --> 00:15:27,881
Then the two worker nodes join in under two minutes.

226
00:15:28,433 --> 00:15:31,019
Then Terraform reaches inside the new cluster:

227
00:15:31,019 --> 00:15:35,348
it creates our namespace and installs the load balancer controller with Helm.

228
00:15:35,900 --> 00:15:38,861
Seventy two resources, in twelve minutes.

229
00:15:38,861 --> 00:15:44,204
Now let us go into AWS and verify that Terraform created what we expected.

230
00:15:45,967 --> 00:15:50,566
This is the AWS console, through a read only session.

231
00:15:50,566 --> 00:15:56,989
Our VPC, eks devsecops, with the address range 10.42 dot 0 dot 0 slash 16.

232
00:15:56,989 --> 00:16:00,026
I have redacted the account number.

233
00:16:00,567 --> 00:16:03,671
The resource map shows the design we read in Terraform:

234
00:16:03,671 --> 00:16:09,201
in each of the two zones, one public and one private subnet, and the route tables connecting them.

235
00:16:10,967 --> 00:16:12,597
The four subnets:

236
00:16:12,597 --> 00:16:17,391
public 48 and 49, private 0 and 16, all available.

237
00:16:17,933 --> 00:16:20,500
Notice the nodes are in private subnets.

238
00:16:20,500 --> 00:16:21,719
Let me explain why.

239
00:16:21,719 --> 00:16:26,211
A node without a public IP cannot be reached from the internet at all,

240
00:16:26,211 --> 00:16:29,419
so a whole class of attacks simply does not apply.

241
00:16:29,419 --> 00:16:34,104
But the nodes still need to reach out, to pull images and to talk to AWS.

242
00:16:34,104 --> 00:16:36,349
That is the job of the NAT gateway:

243
00:16:36,349 --> 00:16:37,248
outbound only.

244
00:16:39,000 --> 00:16:40,452
The EKS cluster:

245
00:16:40,452 --> 00:16:48,260
active, Kubernetes 1.37, standard support until December 2027, and zero health issues.

246
00:16:48,800 --> 00:16:53,525
In the details you can see the API server endpoint and the cluster IAM role.

247
00:16:53,525 --> 00:16:57,814
Every A R N here contains the account number, which you see redacted.

248
00:16:59,567 --> 00:17:00,939
The access tab.

249
00:17:00,939 --> 00:17:02,769
Authentication mode:

250
00:17:02,769 --> 00:17:03,501
EKS API.

251
00:17:03,501 --> 00:17:08,076
That means access entries, not the old config map.

252
00:17:08,633 --> 00:17:09,480
Four entries:

253
00:17:09,480 --> 00:17:13,779
the EKS service role, the node role, my admin user from Terraform,

254
00:17:13,779 --> 00:17:17,232
and our GitHub deploy role with only the edit policy.

255
00:17:17,232 --> 00:17:19,903
On the command line we can see the scope:

256
00:17:19,903 --> 00:17:24,397
the deploy role's edit policy applies to one namespace, profile card.

257
00:17:24,397 --> 00:17:26,221
Nothing else in the cluster.

258
00:17:27,967 --> 00:17:29,068
The node group:

259
00:17:29,068 --> 00:17:36,041
two t3 medium nodes, Amazon Linux 2023, minimum two, maximum three, in our two private subnets.

260
00:17:36,041 --> 00:17:38,096
And look at the blue banner:

261
00:17:38,096 --> 00:17:42,206
my console identity has no access to Kubernetes objects.

262
00:17:42,206 --> 00:17:43,381
That is correct.

263
00:17:43,381 --> 00:17:49,326
A read only AWS identity without an access entry sees nothing inside the cluster.

264
00:17:49,867 --> 00:17:53,210
The launch template confirms the security settings:

265
00:17:53,210 --> 00:17:59,306
instance metadata tokens required, a hop limit of one, and an encrypted 20 gigabyte gp3 disk.

266
00:18:01,067 --> 00:18:06,683
In EC2 you can see the two running nodes, one in eu-central-1a and one in 1b.

267
00:18:06,683 --> 00:18:09,017
The public IPv4 column is empty.

268
00:18:09,017 --> 00:18:11,861
No node is reachable from the internet.

269
00:18:13,600 --> 00:18:14,428
The KMS key:

270
00:18:14,428 --> 00:18:17,947
customer managed, enabled, with automatic rotation.

271
00:18:17,947 --> 00:18:19,810
Why a customer managed key?

272
00:18:19,810 --> 00:18:24,295
Because you control its policy and you can audit every use of it.

273
00:18:24,295 --> 00:18:29,815
If a Kubernetes secret is ever read from etcd storage directly, it is encrypted.

274
00:18:30,367 --> 00:18:31,996
The container registry:

275
00:18:31,996 --> 00:18:34,616
tag immutability on, scan on push on.

276
00:18:34,616 --> 00:18:38,866
Nobody can silently replace an image behind an existing tag.

277
00:18:39,400 --> 00:18:41,832
And CloudWatch holds two log groups:

278
00:18:41,832 --> 00:18:47,372
the cluster's control plane logs, and the VPC flow logs, both kept for seven days.

279
00:18:47,372 --> 00:18:50,884
In an incident, these are the first places you look.

280
00:18:52,633 --> 00:18:56,379
Let me summarise the cluster security at a senior level.

281
00:18:56,379 --> 00:18:57,315
Private nodes:

282
00:18:57,315 --> 00:19:01,663
no public IP, so nothing on the internet can even try to connect.

283
00:19:02,200 --> 00:19:04,866
IMDS version two with a hop limit of one:

284
00:19:04,866 --> 00:19:08,507
every node has AWS credentials in its instance metadata.

285
00:19:08,507 --> 00:19:11,758
The hop limit means a container cannot reach them,

286
00:19:11,758 --> 00:19:15,724
so a compromised pod cannot become a compromised AWS account.

287
00:19:16,267 --> 00:19:17,435
Encrypted disks:

288
00:19:17,435 --> 00:19:21,526
if a volume snapshot is ever copied, it reveals nothing.

289
00:19:22,067 --> 00:19:27,416
KMS envelope encryption of Kubernetes secrets, with a key you control and can audit.

290
00:19:27,967 --> 00:19:28,912
Access entries:

291
00:19:28,912 --> 00:19:35,401
every identity that can use the cluster is listed explicitly, and the pipeline only gets one namespace.

292
00:19:35,967 --> 00:19:37,176
And Pod Identity:

293
00:19:37,176 --> 00:19:43,647
when a pod needs AWS permissions, like the load balancer controller, it gets them from EKS.

294
00:19:43,647 --> 00:19:47,843
We never put AWS access keys inside the Kubernetes cluster.

295
00:19:49,600 --> 00:19:51,867
Now the load balancer controller.

296
00:19:51,867 --> 00:19:52,966
What does it do?

297
00:19:52,966 --> 00:20:00,933
It watches Kubernetes Ingress objects, and for each one it creates and manages a real AWS Application Load Balancer,

298
00:20:00,933 --> 00:20:04,368
with listeners, target groups and security groups.

299
00:20:04,368 --> 00:20:06,634
Terraform installed it with Helm:

300
00:20:06,634 --> 00:20:09,245
chart and app version 3.5.0, deployed.

301
00:20:09,800 --> 00:20:13,624
It runs as two replicas in kube-system, both running.

302
00:20:14,167 --> 00:20:18,461
And how does it get AWS permissions to create load balancers?

303
00:20:18,461 --> 00:20:20,362
A Pod Identity association:

304
00:20:20,362 --> 00:20:23,531
its service account is linked to an IAM role.

305
00:20:23,531 --> 00:20:26,347
EKS hands the pod temporary credentials.

306
00:20:26,347 --> 00:20:28,600
There is no access key anywhere.

307
00:20:29,133 --> 00:20:30,343
And the nodes:

308
00:20:30,343 --> 00:20:34,492
two, ready, Kubernetes 1.37, and no external IP.

309
00:20:34,492 --> 00:20:38,123
The platform is ready for our application.

310
00:20:39,867 --> 00:20:41,851
Now the application manifests.

311
00:20:41,851 --> 00:20:45,886
Two replicas, and a rolling update with max unavailable zero:

312
00:20:45,886 --> 00:20:49,326
an old pod is only removed after a new one is ready.

313
00:20:49,867 --> 00:20:55,101
No service account token, because the app never calls the Kubernetes API.

314
00:20:55,101 --> 00:21:02,271
It runs as user 10101, never root, with the default seccomp profile blocking dangerous system calls.

315
00:21:02,833 --> 00:21:05,347
The container cannot escalate privileges,

316
00:21:05,347 --> 00:21:09,823
its root file system is read only, and every Linux capability is dropped.

317
00:21:10,367 --> 00:21:15,408
Resource requests reserve what it needs, and limits stop it from starving its neighbours.

318
00:21:15,967 --> 00:21:21,727
Readiness and liveness probes on the healthz endpoint, so traffic only reaches pods that are ready.

319
00:21:21,727 --> 00:21:27,661
And a fifteen second pause before stopping, so the load balancer can drain a pod before it disappears.

320
00:21:29,400 --> 00:21:30,581
Network policies.

321
00:21:30,581 --> 00:21:32,039
First a default deny:

322
00:21:32,039 --> 00:21:36,484
no traffic in and no traffic out for every pod in the namespace.

323
00:21:37,033 --> 00:21:38,921
Then exactly one exception:

324
00:21:38,921 --> 00:21:42,766
TCP port 8080, and only from the load balancer subnets.

325
00:21:42,766 --> 00:21:48,220
Not from the whole VPC, because on EKS, pod addresses come from the VPC range,

326
00:21:48,220 --> 00:21:51,926
and that would let every other pod in the cluster in.

327
00:21:51,926 --> 00:21:55,351
I learned that the hard way in my previous build.

328
00:21:55,900 --> 00:22:00,371
A pod disruption budget keeps at least one pod running during node maintenance.

329
00:22:00,933 --> 00:22:06,803
The Ingress asks for an internet facing Application Load Balancer that sends traffic straight to pod IPs,

330
00:22:06,803 --> 00:22:08,760
and checks their health on healthz.

331
00:22:09,300 --> 00:22:12,226
And the namespace itself, created by Terraform,

332
00:22:12,226 --> 00:22:16,210
enforces the Kubernetes Pod Security standard called restricted.

333
00:22:16,210 --> 00:22:19,944
Pods that do not follow these rules are refused at the door.

334
00:22:21,700 --> 00:22:28,310
Here is the difference between the application works, and the application is running securely.

335
00:22:28,310 --> 00:22:32,037
Inside a running pod, the process runs as user 10101.

336
00:22:32,037 --> 00:22:32,669
Not root.

337
00:22:33,233 --> 00:22:37,415
If an attacker gets in, they cannot change the website files:

338
00:22:37,415 --> 00:22:38,923
read only file system.

339
00:22:38,923 --> 00:22:41,803
Only a small temporary folder is writable.

340
00:22:42,367 --> 00:22:44,882
The process has zero Linux capabilities.

341
00:22:44,882 --> 00:22:48,151
Both the permitted and the effective sets are empty.

342
00:22:48,700 --> 00:22:52,327
It cannot call out to the internet, not even resolve a name,

343
00:22:52,327 --> 00:22:55,591
so an attacker cannot download tools or send data out.

344
00:22:56,133 --> 00:22:59,764
And nobody can start a privileged container in this namespace.

345
00:22:59,764 --> 00:23:01,169
Pod Security refuses it.

346
00:23:01,169 --> 00:23:07,318
A container that only works when it runs as privileged root is a problem waiting to happen in production.

347
00:23:09,067 --> 00:23:10,261
Now the container.

348
00:23:10,261 --> 00:23:12,252
The Dockerfile has two stages:

349
00:23:12,252 --> 00:23:17,960
Node builds the static files, and the final image only contains nginx and those files.

350
00:23:17,960 --> 00:23:19,819
Notice the apk upgrade step.

351
00:23:19,819 --> 00:23:28,911
In my first build, Trivy blocked the official base image because of a high severity bug in a library called pcre2 that already had a fix.

352
00:23:28,911 --> 00:23:31,500
Upgrading the Alpine packages fixed it.

353
00:23:32,033 --> 00:23:38,113
Trivy scans the operating system packages and the application dependencies inside the image,

354
00:23:38,113 --> 00:23:40,690
against public vulnerability databases.

355
00:23:40,690 --> 00:23:46,109
Critical or high with a fix available means exit code one, and the pipeline stops.

356
00:23:46,109 --> 00:23:46,439
Here:

357
00:23:46,439 --> 00:23:46,770
zero.

358
00:23:46,770 --> 00:23:50,008
But remember, scanning only finds known problems.

359
00:23:50,008 --> 00:23:54,105
That is why it is one layer among many, not the whole defence.

360
00:23:55,867 --> 00:24:00,529
An SBOM tells us what components exist inside our image.

361
00:24:00,529 --> 00:24:02,610
Syft lists every package:

362
00:24:02,610 --> 00:24:09,604
curl, musl, nginx, zlib, and pcre2 version 10.49, the fixed version from that story.

363
00:24:10,167 --> 00:24:13,818
Seventy packages in total, written as a CycloneDX document.

364
00:24:13,818 --> 00:24:20,563
The day a new vulnerability is announced, you search your SBOMs and know in seconds whether you are affected.

365
00:24:22,300 --> 00:24:24,945
And the last piece before the registry:

366
00:24:24,945 --> 00:24:25,488
signing.

367
00:24:25,488 --> 00:24:28,201
Signing means a cryptographic statement:

368
00:24:28,201 --> 00:24:31,660
this exact image digest was built by this pipeline.

369
00:24:31,660 --> 00:24:33,694
Image provenance, in one word.

370
00:24:34,233 --> 00:24:36,095
We use cosign in keyless mode.

371
00:24:36,095 --> 00:24:41,680
The GitHub job proves its identity, gets a certificate valid for a few minutes, and signs.

372
00:24:41,680 --> 00:24:45,155
There is no long lived signing key that could be stolen,

373
00:24:45,155 --> 00:24:48,878
and every signature is written to a public transparency log.

374
00:24:49,433 --> 00:24:53,604
That is why signing happens inside the pipeline, not on my laptop:

375
00:24:53,604 --> 00:24:56,701
the identity being signed is the pipeline itself.

376
00:24:56,701 --> 00:24:58,723
You will see it run in part two.

377
00:24:59,267 --> 00:25:00,844
And the honest next step:

378
00:25:00,844 --> 00:25:04,881
in this project the cluster does not yet refuse unsigned images.

379
00:25:04,881 --> 00:25:08,224
My previous project shows exactly that, with Kyverno.

380
00:25:08,224 --> 00:25:11,946
That is where signing turns from evidence into enforcement.

381
00:25:13,700 --> 00:25:14,803
That is part one.

382
00:25:14,803 --> 00:25:18,046
We have a VPC across two zones with private nodes,

383
00:25:18,046 --> 00:25:22,067
an EKS cluster with encryption, audit logs and access entries,

384
00:25:22,067 --> 00:25:27,451
the load balancer controller without any keys, and a workload that runs restricted.

385
00:25:27,451 --> 00:25:31,991
And we verified all of it, on the command line and in the AWS console.

386
00:25:32,533 --> 00:25:35,279
In part two, we connect GitHub through OIDC,

387
00:25:35,279 --> 00:25:40,021
push code through the real pipeline, watch a security check fail on purpose,

388
00:25:40,021 --> 00:25:44,514
troubleshoot a broken rollout, and then destroy everything and prove it.

389
00:25:44,514 --> 00:25:45,388
See you there.
